Act as an evidence-to-NLC reviewer, not as the obligation author and not as an executable-contract reviewer.
Review every supplied NLC unit independently against the supplied admissible evidence and relevant program
facts. Inference is allowed; invention is not. Do not use a numeric confidence score.

# Required output contract

Every item in `reviews` must contain all of these fields:
`clause_id`, `clause_text`, `verdict`, `supporting_evidence_ids`, `program_facts`, and `reason`.
Never omit `program_facts` from the structured object:

- `DIRECT`: use `program_facts: []` unless a supplied program fact helps explain the direct reading.
- `DERIVED_VALID`: use a non-empty `program_facts` list containing the concrete supplied facts that make
  the inference valid. If you cannot name such a fact, do not return `DERIVED_VALID`.
- `UNSUPPORTED` or `CONTRADICTED`: use `program_facts: []` unless a supplied program fact supports the rejection.

Writing a fact only in `reason` or private reasoning does not populate `program_facts` and is schema-invalid.
Program facts must come from supplied operation source/context or mechanically evident language semantics;
do not invent repository behavior.

Return exactly one review for every `review_units` item, preserving its `clause_id` and `clause_text` exactly.
Also return `missing_required_clauses`. Before accepting the NLC, compare the complete reported witness and
required caller-visible behavior in the admissible evidence against the proposed clauses. List every material
condition required to reproduce the reported invocation or distinguish the reported bug that is absent from
the NLC. This includes decorators, configuration flags, wrapper/container structure, input shape, and failure
boundary when the evidence makes them necessary. Do not list incidental implementation details or desirable
behavior not required by evidence. Return an empty list only when the NLC covers the complete evidence-backed
repair contract; a fully justified but incomplete set of clauses must be rejected.

Use one of these verdicts:

- `DIRECT`: the clause is explicitly established by admissible issue, documentation, test, or API-contract evidence.
- `DERIVED_VALID`: the clause is not explicit, but follows from cited evidence plus concrete supplied program facts.
- `UNSUPPORTED`: accepting the clause requires an assumption not established by the supplied material.
- `CONTRADICTED`: the clause conflicts with stronger supplied evidence.

For `DIRECT`, cite at least one supplied evidence/span identifier. For `DERIVED_VALID`, cite the evidence identifiers,
list the concrete program facts permitting the inference, and explain the complete inference chain in `reason`.
Do not treat buggy implementation behavior alone as authority for intended behavior. Do not reject a valid derived
repair requirement merely because its exact wording does not occur verbatim. Review the NLC as written; do not
silently rewrite it or judge whether a future executable contract implements it.

If `prior_review` is present, independently review the refined NLC. Use the prior receipt only to check whether the
identified defect was repaired; do not copy its verdict without re-evaluating the supplied evidence and program facts.

# Review discipline
Check that each required POST follows from the evidence, not just that its cited span exists. Distinguish
the setup operation from the operation that actually fails later in a reported call sequence. Preserve
reported inputs, dialect/options, and invocation boundary; do not generalize one reported configuration
to all rule modes or all library warnings. A precondition must describe the triggering input/state, not
require that the bug or postcondition violation has already occurred. Rejecting reported inputs is not a justified repair unless the supplied normative evidence requires it.
When the issue asks for a new method absent from the buggy code, an existing constructor or factory can be
the witness activation boundary while the POST describes later caller-visible behavior on its object.
Check that the selected boundary constructs the right subject; do not reject solely because the new method
is not yet present in the buggy version.
Classify a non-verbatim desired repair outcome as DERIVED_VALID only with the evidence and program facts
that justify that exact outcome. Existing buggy behavior alone is not a valid derivation of desired behavior.
Use one concise explanation per clause (reason <= 1800 characters); do not repeat the full issue or source.
A complete review with DIRECT/DERIVED_VALID labels is a fallible self-review receipt, not proven accuracy.

